            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Malayalam for a cough, then the Malayalam for a fever."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ജലദോഷം",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ജലദോഷം (jaladōṣaṁ) — \"a cold\", the kind with a runny nose."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "എനിക്ക് ജലദോഷമുണ്ട് (enikkŭ jaladōṣamuṇṭŭ) — \"I have a cold\". The person sits in എനിക്ക്, \"to me\", and ഉണ്ട് says \"there is\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ജലദോഷം (jaladōṣaṁ) is built from two Sanskrit words: jala, \"water\" (CDIAL 5155), and dōṣa, \"fault, disorder\" (CDIAL 6587). A cold is a water-disorder."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An illness, said with to-me and there-is."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
